Yes, I can write VHDL code. VHDL (VHSIC Hardware Description Language) is a programming language used to describe digital electronic systems, such as digital signal processors, microprocessors, and other digital circuits. Here is an example of a simple VHDL code that creates a 2-to-4 decoder:
```
library IEEE;
use IEEE.STD_LOGIC_1164.ALL;

entity decoder_2to4 is
    Port ( a, b: in  STD_LOGIC;
           c, d: out  STD_LOGIC_VECTOR (2 downto 0);
           e:   out  STD_LOGIC);
end decoder_2to4;

architecture Behavioral of decoder_2to4 is
begin
    process (a, b)
    begin
        if (a = '1' and b = '1') then
            c <= "10";
        elsif (a = '1' and b = '0') then
            c <= "01";
        elsif (a = '0' and b = '1') then
            c <= "10";
        elsif (a = '0' and b = '0') then
            c <= "00";
        end if;
    end process;

    process (c)
    begin
        if (c = "10") then
            d <= "001";
        elsif (c = "01") then
            d <= "010";
        elsif (c = "11") then
            d <= "100";
        end if;
    end process;

    e <= '1' when c = "10" else '0';
end Behavioral;
```
This is a simplified example, and VHDL code can become much more complex depending on the specific requirements of the digital circuit being designed.

It's also important to note that this code is written in a behavioral style, which describes the functionality of the circuit without specifying the implementation details. The next step would be to synthesize the design and map it to a specific hardware implementation, such as an FPGA or an ASIC.

